RANK: CURVBEND-NORM-RESIDUAL | position=1 | reason=E6 shows the recoverable headroom is the depth2->depth3 prefix collapse (gold-prefix survival 0.829->0.161 while l1_br_euclid already scores 0.06207), and this idea acts only on the residual entering levels 2-3 with the coarse-referenced z_1-z_l profile, preserves the residual norm and the codebook bit-for-bit, and explicitly enumerates the behaviour-off/geometry-off/both-on switches, so it matches the observed failure with the least structural downside.
RANK: CURVCHART-RESGUARD | position=2 | reason=Resolution is protected by construction (code vectors, count and radius untouched) and the [0.5,2]*q_e^0 guard band is derived (rho_l from epoch-0 residual RMS, c0=tanh(1)^2/rho_l^2), which suits E11's real but weak prefix structure (same_first_prefix_rate 0.0907 vs permuted null 0.067); it is held below first because its spec never enumerates the three switches and its per-code Ollivier mean is noise-prone on small cells, so the expected assignment change may stay under the 0.5% no-op threshold it sets.
RANK: CURVCAP-SUCCPLAN | position=3 | reason=The coupling is genuine (successor-overlap sets per-pair curvature in the transport cost) and the frozen Euclidean column marginals do guarantee the baseline 247/7414/20223 resolution, but freezing to the Euclidean nearest-code counts makes curvature a pure permutation that can be a near-no-op, it locks in the K=256 level-1 granularity that E8 finds inferior to smaller K (0.3106 at K=16 vs 0.0822 at K=256), and it adds ~1.0-1.8x step cost for that risk.
RANK: CURVCHART-EQUIMARGIN-RQ | position=4 | reason=Its hard uniform marginals force all 256 codes alive (4 items/code/step), which is positively contradicted by E8 (small K keeps behavioural neighbours together 0.3106 vs 0.0822 at K=256) and by E5's 2.8-44.3 spread of successor support, its alpha-decay safeguard can never fire because U_l is pinned to K, and it gambles the coarse partition the most, so it is ranked last despite enumerating the switches.